    "https://facultycareers.gsu.edu/postings/7556": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor of Criminal Justice and Criminology - Tenured",
      "description": "Georgia State University |Applicant Portal | Associate or Full Professor of Criminal Justice and Criminology - Tenured Skip to Main Content Perimeter College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Home Georgia State University Academics Office of Faculty Affairs Associate or Full Professor of Criminal Justice and Criminology - Tenured Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Interested individuals should submit (1) a letter of interest describing their qualifications for the position, including their accomplishments and future plans in research, teaching, graduate student mentoring, and service (maximum 3 pages); (2) a curriculum vitae; (3) up to three representative publications. Candidates can provide a URL in the \"Media File\" section or Document examples in one file no larger than 10 MB in the 'Other Document 1' section; and (4) the names and contact information provided in the application should be for those who have agreed to provide letters of recommendation. Reference letters will be requested at the time of screening interviews. All materials should be submitted online Position Details Position Information Posting Number F00616P Classification Title Open Rank Tenure-Track Faculty Position Title Associate or Full Professor of Criminal Justice and Criminology - Tenured GSU Information Georgia State University is a national model for innovation and opening opportunities for all, among the top five most innovative by U.S. News & World Report, ranked 1st among public universities in undergraduate teaching, the number 1 nonprofit university to award bachelor’s degrees to African American students, 4th in the country for its first-year student experience, and highly ranked for enabling the social and economic mobility of its students. Among the most diverse universities in the nation, Georgia State opens global perspectives as the number 1 U.S. public university sending African American students to study abroad. A regional gateway to higher education, the five suburban Perimeter College campuses provide more than 30 associate degree pathways. The downtown Atlanta campus offers more than 250 degree programs in 100 fields of study at the undergraduate, graduate and first-professional degree levels. With five university-wide research centers and numerous centers and institutes at the college and school levels, Georgia State’s interdisciplinary, dynamic and productive research enterprises address major health and societal challenges. It is one of only 146 universities with an R1 designation from the Carnegie Foundation<https://www.carnegiefoundation.org/>, an honor reserved for the nation’s most active research institutions. Outgoing research expenditures have doubled in the last decade and topped $1.2 billion over the last six years, an indication of the University’s rapid research growth. Georgia State is vital to the regional economy, with a more than $2.9 billion economic impact, and is also a vital part of Atlanta’s vibrant arts and entertainment community, with highly regarded programs in music, art, creative writing and film, the Rialto Center for the Arts, and the Creative Media Industries Institute that contributes to innovation and the workforce for interactive media and media entrepreneurship. General information on faculty roles and responsibilities can be obtained from the Faculty Handbook https://faculty.gsu.edu/faculty-handbook/ and the Office of Faculty Affairs website https://faculty.gsu.edu/. All faculty at Georgia State are, as part of their essential job duties, expected to: (1) be physically present on-campus as needed to contribute to Georgia State’s in-person educational experience, and (2) be able to teach in each of the following instructional modalities – in-person, online, or blended/hybrid – as determined by their Department Chair or Dean. Department Criminal Justice & Criminology College Andrew Young School of Policy Studies Campus Atlanta Position Type Full Time Faculty Posting Type Internal/External About the Department The Department of Criminal Justice and Criminology at Georgia State University’s Andrew Young School of Policy Studies is nationally recognized for its innovative research, teaching, and service in criminology, criminal justice, and related policy areas. Located in downtown Atlanta, the department leverages its proximity to major justice agencies and research institutions to engage with pressing local and national issues in crime and justice policy. Offering degrees at the bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral levels, as well as minors in Criminal Justice and Digital Criminology, the department prepares students to advance the public good through evidence-based practice and leadership. Faculty are deeply involved in cutting-edge research and public service initiatives, working closely with federal, state, and local partners, and leading major centers such as the Evidence-Based Cybersecurity Research Group and the Leadership Development Institute of Georgia. The department also houses two peer-reviewed journals, including Criminal Justice Review and International Criminal Justice Review , and plays a key role in university-wide research collaborations and digital learning initiatives, ensuring that students and scholars alike are equipped to shape the future of justice policy and practice. Position Description The Department of Criminal Justice and Criminology at Georgia State University invites applications for an associate or early-career full professor position beginning in August 2027. We are particularly interested in scholars whose work engages one or more of the following: crime and justice policy; cybersecurity, cybercrime, and artificial intelligence; inequalities in crime and criminal justice; law enforcement; or violence and victims. This is a nine-month appointment that includes an active research program; undergraduate and graduate teaching, including in-person instruction; graduate student mentoring and dissertation supervision; and service responsibilities. Candidates for appointment at the rank of associate professor should demonstrate a sustained record of scholarly contributions and sponsored research appropriate to the rank, along with evidence of effective university teaching and successful graduate student mentoring. Candidates for appointment at the rank of professor should demonstrate a distinguished and sustained record of scholarly contributions appropriate to the rank, along with evidence of effective university teaching. A successful and sustained record of external research funding, including service as principal investigator or co-principal investigator on sponsored projects, and a demonstrated record of mentoring doctoral students is required. Application review will begin on October 31 and will continue until the position is filled. This position is located on the Atlanta campus of Georgia State University and requires in-person, on-campus work. The Andrew Young School maintains a flexible work environment. Required Experience Candidates for appointment at the rank of associate professor should demonstrate a sustained record of scholarly contributions and sponsored research appropriate to the rank, along with evidence of effective university teaching and successful graduate student mentoring. Candidates for appointment at the rank of professor should demonstrate a distinguished and sustained record of scholarly contributions appropriate to the rank, along with evidence of effective university teaching. A successful and sustained record of external research funding, including service as principal investigator or co-principal investigator on sponsored projects, and a demonstrated record of mentoring doctoral students is required. Required Education Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in Criminal Justice, Criminology, or a substantively related field. Preferred Qualifications/Experience We are especially interested in a colleague whose research agenda shows strong potential to contribute to interdisciplinary work across the Andrew Young School, including in Policy Analytics, Urban Policy, and Computational Social Science, and engagement with community partners, and whose work advances the goals of the school’s strategic plan, Leading with Purpose . Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Licenses, Certifications, or Registrations, etc Pre-Employment Requirements A criminal background check is required. Work Hours Salary TBD Search Chair Contact info All inquiries about the position and submission requirements should be directed to: Dr. David Maimon, Search Committee Chair, Department of Criminal Justice and Criminology, Georgia State University, Atlanta, GA. E-mail: dmaimon@gsu.edu. Departmental contact for assistance Alex Nguyen, Business Manager. Email: anguyen64@gsu.edu Job Duties Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies 1 Desired Start Date 08/01/2027 Position End Date (if temporary) Open Date 10/29/2026 Review of Applications Begins Close Date 11/30/2026 Open Until Filled Yes Special Instructions Summary Interested individuals should submit (1) a letter of interest describing their qualifications for the position, including their accomplishments and future plans in research, teaching, graduate student mentoring, and service (maximum 3 pages); (2) a curriculum vitae; (3) up to three representative publications. Candidates can provide a URL in the “Media File” section or Document examples in one file no larger than 10 MB in the ‘Other Document 1’ section; and (4) the names and contact information provided in the application should be for those who have agreed to provide letters of recommendation. Reference letters will be requested at the time of screening interviews. All materials should be submitted online Apply at: https://facultycareers.gsu.edu/postings/7528 Equal Opportunity Employment Statement Georgia State University is an equal opportunity employer with regard to veterans and individuals with disabilities and prohibits discrimination on the basis of an individual’s race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, or protected veteran status. USG Core Values Statement The University System of Georgia is comprised of our 26 institutions of higher education and learning, as well the System Office. Our USG Statement of Core Values are Integrity, Excellence, Accountability, and Respect. These values serve as the foundation for all that we do as an organization, and each USG community member is responsible for demonstrating and upholding these standards. More details on the USG Statement of Core Values and Code of Conduct are available in USG Board Policy 8.2.18.1.2 and can be found on-line at https://www.usg.edu/policymanual/section8/C224/#p8.2.18_personnel_conduct. Additionally, USG supports Freedom of Expression as stated in Board Policy 6.5 Freedom of Expression and Academic Freedom found on-line at https://www.usg.edu/policymanual/section6/C2653 . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). How did you hear about this employment opportunity? Public Job Posting Internal Job Posting Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Website Chronicle of Higher Education/Vitae HigherEdJobs.com Computing Research Association CommunityCollegeJobs.com Diverse Jobs AcademicJobsOnline.org Other DiversityinAcademia.net Indeed Circa Works Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Resume/CV Letter of Intent/Cover Letter Teaching Evaluations Optional Documents Media File Other Document 1 Other Document 2 Other Document 3 Georgia State University | 33 Gilmer Street SE Atlanta, GA 30303 | 404-413-2000 Contact Georgia State View legal statement Privacy Notices State Authorization Ethics Hotline Website Feedback © 2022 Georgia State University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://facultycareers.gsu.edu/postings/7554": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Criminal Justice and Criminology – Tenure-Track",
      "description": "Georgia State University |Applicant Portal | Assistant Professor of Criminal Justice and Criminology – Tenure-Track Skip to Main Content Perimeter College Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Home Georgia State University Academics Office of Faculty Affairs Assistant Professor of Criminal Justice and Criminology – Tenure-Track Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Interested individuals should submit (1) a curriculum vitae, (2) a letter of interest that includes research interests and experience; a statement of teaching philosophy, experience andeffectiveness, and any relevant service activities, (3) teaching evaluations. ABD applicants should include a detailed update on their dissertation progress. Please note: The names and contact information provided in the application should be for three persons who have agreed to provide letters of recommendation. Position Details Position Information Posting Number F00586P Classification Title Assistant Professor Position Title Assistant Professor of Criminal Justice and Criminology – Tenure-Track GSU Information Georgia State University is a national model for innovation and opening opportunities for all, among the top five most innovative by U.S. News & World Report, ranked 1st among public universities in undergraduate teaching, the number 1 nonprofit university to award bachelor’s degrees to African American students, 4th in the country for its first-year student experience, and highly ranked for enabling the social and economic mobility of its students. Among the most diverse universities in the nation, Georgia State opens global perspectives as the number 1 U.S. public university sending African American students to study abroad. A regional gateway to higher education, the five suburban Perimeter College campuses provide more than 30 associate degree pathways. The downtown Atlanta campus offers more than 250 degree programs in 100 fields of study at the undergraduate, graduate and first-professional degree levels. With five university-wide research centers and numerous centers and institutes at the college and school levels, Georgia State’s interdisciplinary, dynamic and productive research enterprises address major health and societal challenges. It is one of only 146 universities with an R1 designation from the Carnegie Foundation<https://www.carnegiefoundation.org/>, an honor reserved for the nation’s most active research institutions. Outgoing research expenditures have doubled in the last decade and topped $1.2 billion over the last six years, an indication of the University’s rapid research growth. Georgia State is vital to the regional economy, with a more than $2.9 billion economic impact, and is also a vital part of Atlanta’s vibrant arts and entertainment community, with highly regarded programs in music, art, creative writing and film, the Rialto Center for the Arts, and the Creative Media Industries Institute that contributes to innovation and the workforce for interactive media and media entrepreneurship. General information on faculty roles and responsibilities can be obtained from the Faculty Handbook https://faculty.gsu.edu/faculty-handbook/ and the Office of Faculty Affairs website https://faculty.gsu.edu/. All faculty at Georgia State are, as part of their essential job duties, expected to: (1) be physically present on-campus as needed to contribute to Georgia State’s in-person educational experience, and (2) be able to teach in each of the following instructional modalities – in-person, online, or blended/hybrid – as determined by their Department Chair or Dean. Department Criminal Justice & Criminology College Andrew Young School of Policy Studies Campus Atlanta Position Type Full Time Faculty Posting Type Internal/External About the Department The Department of Criminal Justice and Criminology at Georgia State University’s Andrew Young School of Policy Studies is nationally recognized for its innovative research, teaching, and service in criminology, criminal justice, and related policy areas. Located in downtown Atlanta, the department leverages its proximity to major justice agencies and research institutions to engage with pressing local and national issues in crime and justice policy. Offering degrees at the bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral levels, as well as minors in Criminal Justice and Digital Criminology, the department prepares students to advance the public good through evidence-based practice and leadership. Faculty are deeply involved in cutting-edge research and public service initiatives, working closely with federal, state, and local partners, and leading major centers such as the Evidence-Based Cybersecurity Research Group and the Leadership Development Institute of Georgia. The department also houses two peer-reviewed journals, including Criminal Justice Review and International Criminal Justice Review , and plays a key role in university-wide research collaborations and digital learning initiatives, ensuring that students and scholars alike are equipped to shape the future of justice policy and practice. Position Description The Department of Criminal Justice and Criminology at Georgia State University invites applications for two tenure-track Assistant Professor positions beginning August 2027. The primary areas of focus for one position are disparities in the criminal justice system, and/or violence and victims. The primary areas of focus for the other position are cybersecurity, cybercrime, and AI; law enforcement; and/or criminal justice policy. For both positions, candidates should demonstrate the ability to conduct rigorous empirical research. These are nine-month appointments and include undergraduate and graduate teaching in both in-person and online formats (with the possibility of summer teaching), and graduate student mentoring. Required Experience Required Education ! A Ph.D. in Criminal Justice, Criminology, or substantively relevant field of study is required. ABDs may apply, but degree must be in hand by the start of employment. A J.D. alone is not sufficient for these positions. Preferred Qualifications/Experience University teaching experience, a demonstrated record of research and publication, and capacity for securing external funding for research are preferred. We especially welcome candidates with a strong interdisciplinary research orientation across the Andrew Young School, including in Policy Analytics, Urban Policy, and Computational Social Science. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Licenses, Certifications, or Registrations, etc Pre-Employment Requirements A criminal background check is required. Work Hours Salary TBD Search Chair Contact info Dr. William Sabol, wsabol@gsu.edu Departmental contact for assistance Alex Nguyen, anguyen64@gsu.edu Job Duties Posting Detail Information Number of Vacancies 2 Desired Start Date 08/01/2027 Position End Date (if temporary) Open Date 10/29/2026 Review of Applications Begins 10/30/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Special Instructions Summary Interested individuals should submit (1) a curriculum vitae, (2) a letter of interest that includes research interests and experience; a statement of teaching philosophy, experience andeffectiveness, and any relevant service activities, (3) teaching evaluations. ABD applicants should include a detailed update on their dissertation progress. Please note: The names and contact information provided in the application should be for three persons who have agreed to provide letters of recommendation. Apply at: https://facultycareers.gsu.edu/postings/7402 Equal Opportunity Employment Statement Georgia State University is an equal opportunity employer with regard to veterans and individuals with disabilities and prohibits discrimination on the basis of an individual’s race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, or protected veteran status. USG Core Values Statement The University System of Georgia is comprised of our 26 institutions of higher education and learning, as well the System Office. Our USG Statement of Core Values are Integrity, Excellence, Accountabillity, and Respect. These values serve as the foundation for all that we do as an organization, and each USG community member is responsible for demonstrating and upholding these standards. More details on the USG Statement of Core Values and Code of Conduct are available in USG Board Policy 8.2.18.1.2 and can be found on-line at https://www.usg.edu/policymanual/section8/C224/#p8.2.18_personnel_conduct. L Additionally, USG supports Freedom of Expression as stated in Board Policy 6.5 Freedom of Expression and Academic Freedom found on-line at https://www.usg.edu/policymanual/section6/C2653 . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? Public Job Posting Internal Job Posting Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Website Chronicle of Higher Education/Vitae HigherEdJobs.com Computing Research Association CommunityCollegeJobs.com Diverse Jobs AcademicJobsOnline.org Other DiversityinAcademia.net Indeed Circa Works Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Resume/CV Letter of Intent/Cover Letter Philosophy of Teaching/Teaching Portfolio Teaching Evaluations Optional Documents Georgia State University | 33 Gilmer Street SE Atlanta, GA 30303 | 404-413-2000 Contact Georgia State View legal statement Privacy Notices State Authorization Ethics Hotline Website Feedback © 2022 Georgia State University To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
